System Architecture Principles Used in This Project
This project implements several key architectural principles and patterns:
1. Clean Architecture (Onion Architecture)
The project follows Clean Architecture principles with clear separation of concerns across layers:
Domain Layer (src/Domain/): Contains business entities, value objects, and domain logic
Application Layer (src/Application/): Contains use cases, DTOs, interfaces, and application services
Infrastructure Layer (src/Infrastructure/): Contains data access, external services, and framework-specific implementations
Web Layer (src/Web/): Contains controllers, middleware, and presentation concerns
Dependency Direction: Dependencies flow inward - Web → Application → Domain, with Infrastructure implementing Application interfaces.
2. CQRS (Command Query Responsibility Segregation)
The project implements CQRS using MediatR:
Commands: Handle write operations (e.g., LoginCommand, CreateUserCommand)
Queries: Handle read operations (e.g., GetUserById, GetUsers)
Handlers: Separate handlers for each command/query
Pipeline Behaviors: Cross-cutting concerns like validation, authorization, logging, and performance monitoring
3. Multi-Tenant Architecture
The project implements a sophisticated multi-tenant system:
Tenant Resolution: Multiple strategies (subdomain, headers, query parameters)
Tenant Context: ITenantContext service for current tenant information
Tenant Interceptors: Automatic tenant ID stamping on entities
Tenant-Aware Entities: BaseTenantEntity and BaseBusinessEntity with tenant isolation
Tenant Middleware: TenantResolutionMiddleware for request-level tenant resolution
4. Domain-Driven Design (DDD)
Entities: Rich domain models with business logic
Value Objects: Immutable objects like Colour
Domain Events: Event-driven architecture with BaseEvent and domain event handling
Aggregates: Business entities that maintain consistency boundaries
Repositories: Data access abstraction
5. Dependency Injection & Inversion of Control
Service Registration: Centralized in DependencyInjection.cs files
Interface Segregation: Small, focused interfaces
Constructor Injection: Used throughout the application
Service Lifetimes: Appropriate scoping (Scoped, Transient, Singleton)
6. Repository Pattern
Generic Repository: IApplicationDbContext for data access
Specific Repositories: IUserRepository, IBranchRepository
Unit of Work: Entity Framework's DbContext as UoW implementation
7. Cross-Cutting Concerns
Validation: FluentValidation with pipeline behaviors
Authorization: Policy-based authorization with AuthorizationBehaviour
Logging: Structured logging with LoggingBehaviour
Performance: Performance monitoring with PerformanceBehaviour
Exception Handling: Centralized exception handling with UnhandledExceptionBehaviour
8. Cloud-Native Architecture
Azure Integration: Bicep templates for infrastructure as code
App Service: Hosted on Azure App Service
SQL Server: Azure SQL Database
Key Vault: Secrets management
Application Insights: Monitoring and telemetry
Redis: Caching and distributed caching
9. API-First Design
OpenAPI/Swagger: Auto-generated API documentation
NSwag: Client code generation
RESTful APIs: Standard HTTP methods and status codes
Problem Details: RFC 7807 compliant error responses
10. Security Architecture
JWT Authentication: Token-based authentication
Role-Based Authorization: ASP.NET Core Identity with custom roles
Super Admin System: Separate admin context and services
Rate Limiting: Tenant-specific rate limiting
Idempotency: Idempotency middleware for safe retries
11. Testing Strategy
Unit Tests: Domain and Application layer tests
Integration Tests: Infrastructure layer tests
Functional Tests: End-to-end API tests
Test Containers: Database testing with SQL containers
12. Microservices-Ready Design
Service Defaults: Common service configuration
Health Checks: Built-in health monitoring
Distributed Caching: Redis integration
Service Discovery: Ready for container orchestration
This architecture provides a robust, scalable, and maintainable foundation for a multi-tenant hotel management system with clear separation of concerns, strong typing, and comprehensive cross-cutting concerns handling.